/** Translations for siman 066 */
export const TRANSLATIONS = {
  mechaber: {
    '1#main': `Things forbidden because of blood. In it are 10 seifim: Blood of beheimah, chayah, and fowl, whether of tamei or tahor species, is forbidden. And likewise blood of a fetus (shelil). But blood of fish and locusts is permitted.`,
    '2#main': `Blood of eggs — if it is known that it is marukem ha'afroach (meaning that the form of the embryo began to take shape in it), one is liable for it. But if it is not known that it is marukem ha'afroach, one is not liable for it; however, the Sages forbade it.`,
    '3#main': `If a speck of blood is found upon it, one discards the blood and eats the rest — and this is when it is found in the chelevon. But if it is found in the chalmon, the entire egg is forbidden. {Rama: And some say that if it is found upon the knot of the chelevon and also outside it — that is, that it spread from the knot — the entire egg is forbidden (Rosh, Rashba, Rashi, Tosafot, and Or Zarua; and the Rosh, Tur, and Rabbeinu Yerucham wrote that one should be stringent like both views). And some are even more stringent: whatever is found upon the knot of the chelevon, even if it did not spread at all, is forbidden (Or Zarua, Long Handbook beginning general rule 42, and Maharai of Speyer). And from this spread the custom in these lands to forbid any egg in which there is blood, and they do not distinguish between chalmon and chelevon. And in a place where they are accustomed to be lenient — to discard the blood and permit the egg in a place where it is permitted — one must take the measure of a peel along with the blood when discarding it (Long Handbook there in the name of Semag).}`,
    '4#main': `Eggs that were beaten in a bowl and blood is found on the chalmon of one of them — even though one discarded the chalmon upon which the blood was found, they are forbidden, because since they are beaten in a bowl the chelevon, which is thin, mixes together and everything is forbidden. But eggs that are broken together in a bowl and were not beaten, and blood is found in the chelevon of one of them — one takes the kosher chalmonim each one by itself and they are permitted; and one leaves that one in which there was blood in the bowl with the chelevon of the rest and discards them. {Rama: And all this is when we know that the blood was in the place that forbids it. But if there is a doubt whether it was in the place that forbids it or not, one should permit the mixture (his own reasoning in explaining the responsum of the Rosh), since in any case one in two is nullified b'dieved. But if the egg alone is forbidden — and likewise if an egg was cooked or roasted and we do not know whether it is in the forbidden place (all from Issur VeHeter; and see Torat Chatat general rule 62 din 3).}`,
    '5#main': `Blood is found in an egg in a place that does not forbid the whole egg, and he did not discard the blood, and he forgot and beat the egg together with the blood — it appears to me that it is permitted.`,
    '6#main': `Blood is found in an egg that one placed on hot food to shrink — since there is no sauce in the place of the egg, all the hot food is permitted.`,
    '7#main': `Strange eggs (meaning that they spoiled and became like spun thread) — even if the hen sat upon them for many days, it is permitted to eat them, provided that one discards the blood.`,
    '8#main': `It is permitted to eat roasted eggs even though they cannot be checked. {Rama: And likewise one need not check eggs for blood, for we rely on the majority of eggs that have no blood (Hagahot Maimoniyot and Long Handbook). Nevertheless, they were accustomed to be stringent when making food with eggs on a day when they examine them for blood.}`,
    '9#main': `Blood of fish — even though it is permitted, if they collected it in a vessel it is forbidden because of mar'it ayin; therefore, if it is recognizable that it is from fish, such as that it has scales, it is permitted.`,
    '10#main': `Human blood — if it separated from him it is forbidden because of mar'it ayin; therefore, if one bit bread with his teeth and blood came from his teeth onto the bread, he must scrape it off; but what is between the teeth — one sucks it out. {Rama: And all blood of fish and human blood — since by law they are permitted — do not forbid their mixture (Maimoniyot chapter 7 of Forbidden Foods in the name of Rashba, and he is in Tosafot HaRosh house 3 gate 5).}`,
  },
  'siftei-kohen': {
    '1#א': `["<b>Chayah and fowl. And likewise blood of koy — one is liable for it.</b> Tur — and this is simple in the Gemara and all the poskim:"]`,
    '1#ב': `["<b>And likewise blood of a fetus.</b> See above beginning of siman 13:"]`,
    '2#א': `["<b>If it is known that it is marukem ha'afroach.</b> And it will be explained shortly how one knows if it is marukem ha'afroach:"]`,
    '2#ב': `["<b>Marukem ha'afroach, etc.</b> Specifically regarding a drop of blood one is liable for it from the Torah when it is known that it is marukem ha'afroach; but if it is full of blood and the place where the embryo began is recognizable, it is only d'rabbanan, as written in siman 86 s.k. 22:"]`,
    '3#א': `["<b>The entire egg is forbidden.</b> Because the embryo begins to form from the entire egg:"]`,
    '3#ב': `["<b>Forbidden.</b> And the Kol Bo wrote that when blood is found on the chalmon, it is forbidden even if it is a day-old egg, for the Sages did not distinguish; and so too the Beit Yosef and Torat Chatat general rule 62 end of din 1 — that wherever it is forbidden there is no distinction between a day-old egg; and such is the custom. And Maharshal Perek 9 siman 120 — because it appears to him that the essential view of Ra'ah below is to be lenient on the chalmon in a day-old egg and to forbid the chelevon; and the view of the later authorities appears to be that one should not distinguish:"]`,
    '3#ג': `["<b>And some are even more stringent, etc.</b> On the knot of the chelevon — a decree lest blood be found on the knot of the chalmon, which is forbidden d'oraisa. There:"]`,
    '3#ד': `["<b>And from this spread, etc.</b> Until 'and they do not distinguish between chelevon and chalmon.' Since there are poskim who hold that the place of the knot is at the blunt end and there are poskim who hold at the pointed end — if so, it has no permission except when found on the sides, and we are not expert as to how far the blunt end and pointed end extend; therefore the custom is to forbid it in every case — so wrote Torat Chatat general rule 62 end of din 1; and Bach and Lev Chayim daf 79b challenged the Rav on this, for certainly everyone is expert in this, for our eyes see that blood on the sides is far from the knot; rather the reason for the custom is according to the law, that we are concerned for the view of Ra'ah, who is more stringent on chelevon than on chalmon not on the knot, as Ran wrote on his name, and so Rokeach siman 367 wrote the version of the Jerusalem Talmud and an old version in our Gemara to be more stringent on chelevon than chalmon, end of his words (also in Beit Yosef and Darkei Moshe they brought Ra'ah's and Rokeach's words briefly — see there). And their words are correct, in that even though Ra'ah and Rokeach brought a correct rationale for their words (and see Ra'ah's words in his book Bedek HaBayit), and so Maharshal Perek 9 siman 119 like Ra'ah's words — that in chelevon it is forbidden by law and in chalmon one should not be lenient against the view of the Geonim — see there:"]`,
    '4#א': `["<b>Eggs that were beaten, etc.</b> See Perishah seif 12 and Derishah:"]`,
    '4#ב': `["<b>And all this, etc.</b> In Torat Chatat there din 3 he brings the view of the poskim that blood of eggs is not forbidden d'oraisa at all, and the view of the poskim that blood of formation (rikum) is forbidden d'oraisa; and he concludes: and in Issur VeHeter general rule 42 it is written that if an egg was cooked or roasted, or one egg was beaten and blood was found in it and it is not known in which place the blood was found — they are accustomed to forbid it; however, if they are still raw and beaten in a bowl and mixed and blood is found and it is not recognizable in which place it was, it is written in Agudah that it is a rabbinic doubt and to be lenient, and such is our practice even if there are only two or three; but if it is recognizable that the blood was in the place of prohibition — they practice according to the responsum of the Rosh, end of his words. And it appears to me that the same applies if it was cooked (and afterward became mixed) with others — if we do not know that the blood was in the place of prohibition, one should permit the mixtures; for there is no distinction between cooked and raw regarding mixtures; and even though it is possible that through cooking it imparts flavor to others, unlike when it is raw — nevertheless it appears that one may rely on the words of those who are lenient. And we say that blood of eggs is d'rabbanan; and as long as we do not know that the blood was in the place that forbids the egg, one may be lenient from the doubt that perhaps it was not in the place that forbids the egg; and because of the drop of blood itself that is absorbed in the egg — there is no reason to forbid, for Rashba wrote in a responsum that blood of eggs forbids only when it is in its visible form, but it does not forbid its mixture — as will be explained shortly; even though wherever the egg is forbidden it forbids its mixture, as stated in the responsum of the Rosh — nevertheless in a case of doubt one may be lenient, end of his words; and so Darkei Moshe. And according to this are his words here in the hagahah — except that he explained more here in the hagahah the reason to be lenient in mixtures than regarding the egg itself; and that is: since in any case in mixtures one in two is nullified; and even though we are accustomed to permit even when there are only two eggs, as Torat Chatat and Darkei Moshe wrote — nevertheless it is called a mixture; and in mixtures we find that one in two is nullified; and moreover that even with two eggs it is permitted by law, as I will write shortly. And this that he concluded 'but if the egg alone is forbidden' — it appears clear: all the more so whatever became mixed, even only one in one, is permitted, because in mixtures we rely on the poskim that blood of eggs is d'rabbanan. And that which the Rav ruled anonymously like the Mechaber in seif 2 — 'if it is known that it is marukem ha'afroach one is liable' — that is because regarding the egg itself we say every doubt stringently; he left matters as they were. And with this is settled what Sefer Lechem challenged on the Rav on daf 179a — see there. And Pri Chadash wrote here words without attention and there is no need to respond to them, as the examiner will see; and Maharshal there and siman 120 wrote that we rule blood of eggs is d'oraisa; therefore where blood is found on the chelevon and it became mixed, whether raw or cooked — everything is forbidden; and where found on the chalmon not on the knot — one takes the egg with the chalmon and discards it and all the rest is permitted, for the essential law is like Ra'ah, end of his words; and so Bach at the end of seif 4 is stringent and wrote that according to what we rule, to be stringent like Ra'ah's view and like the Geonim's view — if so, in every case of mixture it is forbidden, as the Rosh wrote in his responsum, end of his words. And for practical halacha, the Rav's and Issur VeHeter's words appear — that even according to those who say blood of eggs is d'oraisa, one may be lenient in mixtures, for we rule from the Torah that min b'mino one in two is nullified and the Sages required sixty; and if it spilled and they cannot know whether there are sixty — it is permitted, for it is a rabbinic doubt and to be lenient, as below siman 98 seif 4 — if so, all the more so here where there is doubt whether there is any prohibition at all, for it is nullified in the majority. And even though from the Rav's words in Torat Chatat and Darkei Moshe it appears that he holds that according to those who say blood of eggs is d'oraisa it is a Torah doubt — the Rav followed the responsum of the Rosh, which implies thus; and that is because specifically when it spilled we say thus, since after it was nullified in the majority the prohibition regarding itself is rabbinic; but not where the doubt is in the prohibition itself and it comes to be rabbinic from another aspect — and for this reason we do not permit a doubt of a terefah animal that became mixed in a majority because of a rabbinic doubt to be lenient, as Darkei Moshe distinguished explicitly in siman 57 in such a case — nevertheless here, since Rambam, Semag, Smak, Agudah, Issur VeHeter, and Maharai in his gates hold that blood of eggs is only d'rabbanan — if so, even without a mixture it would be a rabbinic doubt and to be lenient; certainly one should consider it in such a case a rabbinic doubt through mixture according to everyone — examine. And even if only one became mixed with one, it appears permitted, for one may say that the permitted egg is more than the forbidden egg; and if so, it is a double doubt — doubt whether there is a forbidden egg here or not; and even if you say there is a prohibition here, perhaps the permitted egg is the majority; and if so, it is forbidden only d'rabbanan, and it is a rabbinic doubt and to be lenient. And in such a case Semak siman 205 wrote, and Beit Yosef brings it siman 83; and so Issur VeHeter general rule 13; and the Rav in Torat Chatat general rule 20 end of din 3 — fish brine where there is oiliness is forbidden from the Torah, and salted fish like herring are permitted because of a double doubt — whether they were salted with tamei fish or not; and even if you say they were salted, perhaps there was no fat in them and then their brine is forbidden only d'rabbanan — until here his words, and it is exactly like this case here — see there. And even when found on the chalmon and it became mixed, it is considered a doubt — perhaps we hold like Ra'ah and his supporters; and likewise when found on the chelevon and it became mixed — perhaps we hold like the Geonim and their supporters; and Bach himself holds that this is a valid double doubt — see there. And even though I wrote in end of siman 57 that in such a case there is no double doubt except regarding a terefah where there are in any case sides to permit — nevertheless here, since there is doubt of prohibition at all, it certainly appears to be a complete double doubt. And moreover, even according to those who say blood of eggs is d'oraisa, and the blood is in the place that forbids it — the egg is not certainly forbidden, but only from doubt that perhaps formation began, as Rashba and Maharshal wrote there — if so, from all these reasons the words of Issur VeHeter and the Rav appear; but what Maharshal was lenient about when found on the chalmon does not appear at all, and it is not the view of the Rosh and all the later authorities. However, when the egg became mixed not min b'mino, one must forbid the mixture, as Maharshal and Bach wrote; and it appears that Issur VeHeter and the Rav also agree to this, for they did not write their words except when it became mixed with other eggs — so it appears to me:"]`,
    '5#א': `["<b>It appears to me that it is permitted.</b> For there are poskim who hold that there is no prohibition in this blood at all, even d'rabbanan; and even according to those who forbid it d'rabbanan — that is only not to eat it in its visible form because of mar'it ayin; but if it became mixed unintentionally — it is permitted; and so Rashba, etc., end of his words. And it is implied that if they mixed intentionally it is forbidden; and so Pri Chadash — and this is for him personally or for one for whom it was nullified on his account, because of a penalty, as below siman 99 seif 5:"]`,
    '5#ב': `["<b>Permitted.</b> It is written in Sefer Lechem (daf 179b) that even according to the custom where they were accustomed to forbid the egg wherever blood is found — specifically when it is still visible and not when it became mixed, for they did not add a stringency upon a stringency, end of his words. And in truth the Rav's words in Darkei Moshe and Torat Chatat appear at first glance thus, since he ruled anonymously here like the Mechaber; but this is not a correct rationale (and the author of Lechem answered: the rationale is that since it is not visible they were not stringent; and this is what I said — they did not add a stringency; and even though by law it is forbidden, the law itself is because of the stringency of a doubt, end of his words) — for what of it that it became mixed? Even if it were visible and they discarded it, the egg would be forbidden; and all the more so according to what was explained, that we rule that the entire egg is forbidden by law in every case. And it appears more that the Rav did not rule anonymously here except in a place where they were not accustomed to forbid the egg ever; and there is a practical difference also in a place where they were accustomed to forbid the egg — if the blood itself became mixed in mixtures afterward, it is permitted. And Pri Chadash's words cannot be reconciled thus:"]`,
    '6#_': `Permitted, etc. For since there is no sauce in the place of the egg, it is no worse than roasting, which forbids only the measure of a peel; and the peel of an egg itself is no less than the measure of a peel. And moreover, even if it were peeled — since the sauce in the pot does not rise above the cover, what is in the pot does not absorb from it, for it is no worse than a piece within the sauce when one did not stir and cover, end of Rashba's words that Beit Yosef brought; and the Rav in Torat Chatat end of general rule 63 wrote on this: it does not appear to permit by a peel, for the egg is forbidden because of itself and is not comparable to a piece that is forbidden only because of what is absorbed in it, and absorbed prohibition does not pass from piece to piece without sauce — until here his words. And it appears that even Rashba said only to permit what is in the pot; but the piece upon which the egg rests is forbidden the measure of removal, as below siman 92 and siman 105 seif 4; and this is what he meant: 'and moreover,' etc. — 'what is in the pot,' etc. — would be forbidden if so; if so, here when it is not peeled everything is permitted. And Rashba's wording in Beit Yosef is parsed more precisely thus — see there. And Maharshal wrote in Perek 9 end of siman 120 on Rashba's aforementioned words: all this is according to his view; but according to what we rule, that even roasted and hot into hot is considered like cooking and forbids up to sixty — here too it forbids up to sixty, end of his words. And from the Rav's words it appears that he agrees when it is not peeled; and it appears his reason is that even though we rule below siman 105 that even with a weak absorbed prohibition we measure sixty — nevertheless it was explained there in s.k. 17 that with absorbed prohibition, a weak flavor does not spread from piece to piece without sauce; and since the egg is within the peel it is considered absorbed prohibition (and Pri Chadash who copied Rashba's reason did not aim well, for we measure sixty). And Maharshal's words appear — for it is implied from Rashba's own words that if he would forbid up to sixty even where there is no sauce it would be forbidden and would not be considered absorbed prohibition what is within the peel; and so appears Issur VeHeter's view below shortly. And one must say that the word 'from the cover' in Rashba's words is a scribal error and should read 'above the egg,' for it deals when the egg is placed in a pot on the food; and so I found in a corrected text. But in Issur VeHeter general rule 42 din 6 he wrote: Rashba answered that in the cover of the hot food in which an egg was found with blood — it does not forbid the hot food except the measure of a peel, end of his words; and specifically when it did not touch the food itself; but if it touched it, sixty is required against the entire egg, as stated above, end of his words. And according to his words one must say that even though we rule below siman 92 seif 5 that a drop that fell on the pot opposite the sauce requires sixty — nevertheless here, since the egg is within the peel and the peel separates, it is like two hot pots touching one another — which is permitted below end of siman 92 (and accordingly it is proven that Issur VeHeter holds like Maharshal above shortly — examine).`,
    '7#א': `["<b>Strange eggs.</b> Meaning that they are not from a male but rather that they are 'suspended from the ground' and are never fertilized, end of Tur and Rashi; and Rashi explained that then even if there is blood in them it is permitted. But the same applies to eggs that come from a male — they are permitted when there is no blood in them, even though they are strange; or when the blood is in a place that does not forbid it, in a place where leniency is practiced — and so Ran, and Beit Yosef brings it — see there:"]`,
    '7#ב': `["<b>It is permitted to eat them, etc.</b> And he wrote in Shibbolei HaLeket in the name of the Geonim that if the eggs remained under the hen for three days they are forbidden because of revulsion, end of his words. And Beit Yosef wrote that this matter has no root in the Talmud, and I also did not see any posek who wrote thus; on the contrary, Rashi wrote that even if the hen sat on them for many days they are permitted, end of his words; and after him the Rav followed in Torat Chatat general rule 62 din 6. And I do not know why they rejected the Geonim's words without reason, for in the Talmud and all the poskim it states: strange eggs — 'a good soul shall eat them' (and Rashi and other commentators explain: one who is not fastidious and whose mind is not repelled by them); and therefore Rashi explained that even if the hen sat on them for many days they are permitted. But certainly because of revulsion they are forbidden to a fastidious person — and so Issur VeHeter general rule 42 din 5 — see there:"]`,
    '10#_': `It does not forbid its mixture. For they themselves are permitted when there is proof in them — all the more so their mixture. There:`,
  },
  'turei-zahav': {
    '2#_': `But the Sages forbade it. And this is according to one resolution in Tosafot Perek Elu Treifot that Beit Yosef brings here — meaning that in every place it is forbidden d'rabbanan; but according to the second resolution there is a distinction, that blood on the knot is forbidden from the Torah, for it is considered a type of flesh since the embryo is formed from it; but in another place there is no prohibition, even d'rabbanan. And it is implied from Tur and Shulchan Aruch that they adopted the stringency of both resolutions. And in Beit Yosef it is written: but Rambam wrote regarding a tahor fowl's egg, etc., end of his words — it appears that he holds that Rambam disagrees with what Tur wrote, that one is liable if it is marukem ha'afroach — meaning from the Torah. And it is difficult, for Tur himself brings Rambam's words in siman 86; and likewise in Shulchan Aruch he brings in both places like Tur. And it appears to me that what he wrote here 'one is liable' refers to the blood, as Beit Yosef wrote in the name of Rashba that it is blood-flesh; but in siman 86 regarding Rambam he deals when there is no blood there, only formation of flesh — and that is d'rabbanan. And Maharshal brought that one should be stringent and say blood of eggs is d'oraisa, and a practical difference regarding doubt — until here. And nevertheless one should be stringent also like the first resolution and forbid the blood in every place d'rabbanan; but in Torat Chatat general rule 62 he wrote in the name of Hagahot Shaarei Dura and Semak and Maharai that it is a rabbinic doubt and to be lenient; and so Issur VeHeter general rule 42 in the name of Agudah — except that he wrote that specifically in a mixture we rely on this; and such is the essential ruling, as Rama also ruled in seif 4 — see what I wrote there:`,
    '3#_': `But if it is found in the chalmon, etc. Their dispute depends on the sugya in this matter. According to Rosh and Tur, it is stated in the Gemara: if blood is found upon it, one discards the blood and eats the rest. R' Yirmiyah said: and this is when it is found on its knot (Rashi: it is the rooster's seed tied at the head of the blunt end of the egg, and from there embryo formation begins; and since it is still there and has not spread through the rest of the egg, therefore one eats the rest; but if found on the chalmon — the entire egg is forbidden, as below). Dosetai taught: they taught only when found on its chelevon; but if found on the chalmon — the entire egg is forbidden, for 'he cast the flaw into the whole of it' — meaning the defect spread through the whole egg; and this regarding the chalmon that forbids the whole — means on the knot of the chalmon, even if it did not spread outside; and the reason that specifically on the knot of the chelevon and it did not spread outside one eats the rest, but if found on the knot of the chalmon we say that it was already on the knot of the chelevon, from where formation begins and spread outside and came to the knot of the chalmon which faces it; but if found on the chalmon not on the knot and it is not recognizable that it spread from the knot — the egg is permitted. And R' Yirmiyah and Dosetai do not argue: R' Yirmiyah teaches that in the chelevon itself there is a distinction — if the blood is on the knot the blood is forbidden and the egg permitted; if it spread outside, the egg too is forbidden; but not on the knot — whether in chelevon or chalmon the egg is permitted. And Dosetai teaches that on the knot of the chalmon it is as if it spread from the knot of the chelevon outside, and the entire egg is forbidden. This is Rashi's view, and it is one view that Rama brings here. But Rif and Rambam explain that Dosetai argues on R' Yirmiyah and holds that in the chalmon there is no distinction between knot or other place, and it is forbidden everywhere; and in the chelevon everywhere the blood is forbidden and the egg permitted — and this is Shulchan Aruch's view in this seif. And Rosh and Tur ruled to be stringent like both views — meaning in the chalmon the egg is forbidden everywhere, and in the chelevon too it is forbidden if the blood spread from the knot outward, and there is no permission for the egg except in chelevon not on the knot, or on its knot and it did not spread. And Rama wrote that our custom is to forbid the egg in every manner; and the reason is explained in Torat Chatat — that we are not expert which place is called the knot. But there is reason to forbid for another reason — there is a view of Ra'ah that the Gemara's version is reversed, that chelevon is worse; and he wrote that we know the embryo is formed from the chelevon and not from the chalmon — for if you break the egg while the embryo is still inside you will not find anything from the chelevon from which it was formed, and the chalmon remains whole; if so, Ra'ah would forbid everywhere — if found on chelevon, forbidden by his view; if found on chalmon, forbidden by Rif and Rambam's view. And Maharshal wrote that such is the halacha like Ra'ah's view:`,
    '4#א': `That he discarded the chalmon, etc. Beit Yosef wrote that the concern of this law is perhaps the blood was in the place that forbids and came here through beating, and we go stringently — blood of eggs is from the Torah; and according to this, whoever holds blood of eggs is d'rabbanan — we go leniently, for perhaps it was not in the place that forbids. And Torat Chatat general rule 42 wrote that his words are incorrect — for even according to those who say d'rabbanan it is forbidden here, since blood was found on the chalmon we are stringent like Rif, that the chalmon is forbidden by law; only that if there is doubt whether on chalmon or chelevon — in that case Beit Yosef's words are correct, end of his words. Maharshal wrote: one should analyze — if they were beaten, how can he take the chalmon upon which the blood was found, for it is already beaten and mixed; certainly it deals after he began to beat them, he saw blood on the chalmon while it was still whole. And he wrote further that this is according to Rosh like Rashi; but according to Rif, who forbids without uprooting reason except because of the chalmon itself being forbidden. And I say further that if they were not beaten and blood was found on the chalmon not on the knot — he should take the egg with the chalmon and discard it and the rest is permitted, since according to Rashi and Ra'ah it is permitted and their words are essential — only that we are stringent like Rif; therefore we are not so concerned to be stringent in mixtures that a little of it mixed with other eggs; but when he already began to beat, even a little — forbidden, for it is a Torah doubt and one should be stringent, end of his words. And see what I will write shortly in this seif from this:`,
    '4#ב': `One takes the kosher chalmonim, etc. For it is impossible to take an egg from its fellow without leaving some of the upper on the lower; therefore it is better that some of the permitted remain in the bowl with the forbidden, than that nothing forbidden remain with the permitted in the bowl — so Hagahot Mordechai wrote, and he resolves why we do not do the opposite:`,
    '4#ג': `Since in any case one in two is nullified. Meaning from the Torah one in two is nullified even wet in wet; only the Sages required sixty, as below siman 98; therefore here, when there is doubt whether it was in the place that forbids it, it is a rabbinic doubt and we go leniently. But if there was no mixture except one in one — this is not a rabbinic doubt, since from the Torah it is not nullified — if the blood were in the place that forbids, it would be a Torah prohibition; if so, it would be a Torah doubt and stringently; therefore Rama mentions one in two, etc. And one may say that even one in two is not nullified — below siman 111 — only that there it deals with an important matter where the Sages decreed it should not be nullified even in sixty; therefore it has no permission except in two mixtures — not so with a beaten egg, whose importance is diminished; therefore we go leniently in a rabbinic doubt. And even according to Maharshal, who ruled like Ra'ah that everywhere is forbidden as I mentioned in his name shortly — nevertheless a doubt whether it was in the place that forbids is relevant here; if we say the essential view is Rif's version, automatically in chelevon not on the knot there is permission; if we say Ra'ah's version, automatically on the chalmon there is permission; only that from the side of stringency, since we cannot decide, we forbid everywhere; but in truth according to the truth there is doubt whether there is prohibition here. And Maharshal himself ruled to be lenient in mixtures, as I mentioned in his name in this seif — that since according to Rashi and Ra'ah it is permitted and their words are essential, only that we are stringent like Rif, etc. — sixty is not required, and as below siman 98 regarding spilled gravy it is called a rabbinic doubt. Yet we still need to explain Rama's words here — for according to his words it is difficult why Rosh wrote in the responsum that Beit Yosef and Tur bring — since the thin chelevon mixes together and everything is forbidden — for there too it deals when it is not clear to us whether it came from chelevon or chalmon, as explained in the responsum Beit Yosef brings, that it is unknown whether it came from chelevon or chalmon, etc.; and if so, he should have permitted because of Rama's reason. And in truth this rationale of one in two being nullified does not help here to say that therefore it is a rabbinic doubt — since there is doubt whether it is in the place that forbids from the Torah, for whoever says blood of eggs is d'oraisa — it is as if it were certainly in the place that forbids, as we find in siman 110 seif 9: wherever the doubt is in its substance, it does not help afterward to say a double doubt through mixtures; and so Issur VeHeter general rule 27 din 10 — if the first mixture was only one in one and afterward a second mixture — all is forbidden, since in the first mixture it was not nullified from the Torah but was a Torah doubt; it does not help afterward for the second mixture to say one in two is nullified, as if definite prohibition fell there, since it is from the Torah if so. If so, here too this blood would be a Torah doubt and be as if it were certainly in the place that forbids, and afterward one in two being nullified does not help, for sixty from the rabbinic level is still required; and it is not comparable to spilled gravy in siman 98 seif 2 where we go leniently — for there there is certainly a Torah prohibition, only that it has remedy through nullification in the majority which helps from the Torah, only that d'rabbanan sixty is required; here we come to be lenient from the side of doubt of Torah prohibition — in such a case we say it is as definite prohibition; and this is a principle in many places — see what I wrote siman 107 seif 1 on Torat Chatat. And that Rama permitted here because one in two is nullified — is because he goes according to his view that Torat Chatat general rule 62 wrote: specifically when it did not become mixed we say stringently that perhaps it was in the place that forbids, for blood of eggs is d'oraisa; but if it became mixed we rely on that resolution of Tosafot that blood of eggs in every place is only d'rabbanan, since many later authorities hold thus — if so, here he ruled properly in mixtures to be lenient, since there is another leniency that one in two is nullified, and there is no Torah doubt of prohibition here, for we may say there is no Torah prohibition at all — unlike the egg alone, where we are stringent like one who says blood of eggs is d'oraisa; and so Issur VeHeter general rule 42 — therefore Rosh's responsum that forbids the mixture is not difficult at all, for he holds blood of eggs is d'oraisa, and therefore one in two being nullified does not help, as I mentioned. And Maharshal holds that even in mixtures we are stringent from the stringency of blood of eggs being d'oraisa, as I copied in his name above; and for practical halacha one should rule like Rama, as Issur VeHeter also wrote thus, that such is the practice — especially since many later authorities are lenient in every such doubt; and therefore I ruled in a case that came before me: a woman made dough kneaded with eggs, and after kneading a drop of blood was found in the shell of one of them, and there were many eggs from that mixture — and I permitted everything for Rama's reason that I mentioned, that it is as if there is only a rabbinic doubt, since even if it were in the place that forbids it, it would be nullified in the majority from the Torah (and likewise) here in doubt we go leniently. And they said to me that it became mixed in the dough and it is a type not in its type requiring sixty from the Torah, as below siman 98 — this is not so, for it also became mixed in kosher eggs, and we say: remove what is not its type as if it were not there, and its type is greater and nullifies it, as below siman 98 — therefore I permitted the aforementioned mixture, end of his words:`,
    '6#_': `All the hot food is permitted. Maharshal wrote: this is according to his view of Rashba; but according to what we rule below, that even roasted and hot into hot is considered cooking and forbids up to sixty — here too it forbids up to sixty, end of his words. And in siman 105 seif 9 I wrote Maharshal's conclusion that it is so; and it appears one should not be stringent here except if the blood is in a place that certainly forbids; but if found in a place that is not forbidden by law, such as on the chalmon not on the knot — one should not be so stringent here to forbid the hot food; and as Maharshal wrote below that he was lenient in other matters — all the more so here, where many hold that hot in hot does not forbid more than the measure of a peel, and the peel of an egg helps here, for a peel is not required:`,
  },
  'baer-heitev': {
    '1#_': `Chayah. Shach wrote in this regard regarding blood of koy:`,
    '2#א': `One is liable. And Shach wrote: specifically regarding a drop of blood; but if it is full of blood and the place where the embryo began is recognizable, it is only d'rabbanan — see above siman 86:`,
    '2#ב': `The Sages. Maharshal wrote that one should be stringent and say blood of eggs is d'oraisa, and a practical difference regarding doubt; and see Rama's words seif 4:`,
    '3#א': `In the chalmon. Shach brought everything in it; and other poskim hold there is no distinction even if it is a day-old egg — not like Maharshal, who distinguishes in this:`,
    '3#ב': `To the chelevon. Taz and Shach wrote that this custom is according to the law, that we are concerned for Ra'ah's view, a posek who holds one must be more stringent on chelevon than chalmon; and Maharshal wrote that his words are essential for halacha:`,
    '4#א': `And one leaves. Taz wrote the reason that it is impossible to take an egg from its fellow without leaving some of the upper on the lower; therefore it is better that some of the permitted remain in the bowl with the forbidden, than that nothing forbidden remain with the permitted that were removed from the bowl. And he wrote further in the name of Maharshal that if they were not beaten and blood was found on the chalmon not on the knot — he should take the egg with the chalmon and discard it, and the rest is permitted; but when he already began to beat, even a little — forbidden, for it is a Torah doubt and stringently:`,
    '4#ב': `The mixture. Shach wrote that it appears from Rama's words that if the egg alone is forbidden, it implies that if it became mixed, even one in one — permitted, because in mixtures we rely on poskim that blood of eggs is d'rabbanan — not like Bach, who wrote that for us, who hold blood of eggs is d'oraisa, it is a Torah doubt; and in my humble opinion it appears that even according to those who say it is from the Torah, one may be lenient in mixtures, for we rule from the Torah min b'mino one in two is nullified and the Sages required sixty; and if it spilled and they cannot know whether there are sixty — permitted, for it is a rabbinic doubt and to be lenient — all the more so here where there is doubt whether there is any prohibition, for it is nullified in the majority. And even though from the Rav's words in Darkei Moshe it appears he holds that according to those who say blood of eggs is d'oraisa it is a Torah doubt — and specifically when spilled we are lenient, since after nullification in the majority the prohibition regarding itself is rabbinic; but not where the doubt is in the prohibition itself and it comes rabbinically from another aspect — and for this reason we do not permit a doubt of a terefah mixed in a majority because of rabbinic doubt to be lenient, as Darkei Moshe wrote explicitly siman 57 — nevertheless here, since Rambam and many poskim hold blood of eggs is only d'rabbanan — if so, even without mixture it is a rabbinic doubt and to be lenient; certainly one should consider it through mixture a rabbinic doubt for everyone. And even if one became mixed with one — permitted, for one may say the permitted egg is more than the forbidden egg; and if so, it is a double doubt — doubt whether there is a forbidden egg here; and even if you say there is prohibition here, perhaps the permitted egg is the majority; and if so, it is forbidden only d'rabbanan, and it is a rabbinic doubt and to be lenient. And even when found on the chalmon and it became mixed, it is considered a doubt — perhaps we hold like Ra'ah and his supporters; and likewise when found on the chelevon and mixed — perhaps we hold like the Geonim and their supporters. And even though I wrote in end of siman 57 that in such a case there is no double doubt except regarding terefah where there are sides to permit — nevertheless here, since there is doubt of prohibition at all, it certainly appears to be a complete double doubt. And moreover, even according to those who say it is from the Torah, the egg is not certainly forbidden, only from doubt that perhaps formation began — if so, from all these reasons it appears one should be lenient, end of his words. And Taz wrote: a case came before me where a woman made dough kneaded from eggs, and after kneading a drop of blood was found in the shell of one, and there were many eggs from that mixture — and I permitted everything, for it is like a rabbinic doubt, since even if it were in the place that forbids it, it would be nullified in the majority from the Torah; and likewise in doubt we go leniently. And they said to me that it became mixed in the dough and it is a type not in its type requiring sixty from the Torah, as below siman 98 — this is not so, for it also became mixed in kosher eggs, and we say: remove what is not its type as if it were not there, and its type is greater and nullifies it, as below siman 98 — therefore I permitted, end of his words:`,
    '5#_': `And forgot. Shach wrote, and it is implied that if they mixed intentionally it is forbidden for him personally or for one for whom it was nullified on his account, because of a penalty. And all this is where they were not accustomed to forbid the egg forever; and there is a practical difference also where they were accustomed to forbid — if the blood itself became mixed in mixtures afterward, it is permitted:`,
    '6#_': `Permitted. For since there is no sauce in the place of the egg, it is no worse than roasting, which forbids only the measure of a peel (and an egg's peel is no less than the measure of a peel). Maharshal wrote: according to what we rule, that even roasted and hot into hot is considered cooking and forbids up to sixty — here too up to sixty. And Taz wrote that nevertheless one should be stringent only if the blood is in a place that certainly forbids (then sixty is required against the entire egg); but if found on the chalmon not on the knot and similar cases — one may be lenient (and likewise if there is doubt whether it is in the place that forbids — a peel suffices, and an egg's peel helps here. Summary of these laws: if the egg became mixed and we do not know whether it is in the place that forbids — if mixed in its type or in its type and not its type — permitted; but in not its type alone — forbidden and sixty required against the egg. And specifically when hot; but when cold — remove the egg and the rest is permitted like a peel). And Shach wrote that from Rama's words it appears he agrees with the Mechaber if the egg is not peeled; and even though we rule in siman 105 that even with weak flavor we measure sixty — nevertheless it was explained there that with absorbed prohibition, weak flavor does not spread from piece to piece without sauce; and the egg in its shell is absorbed prohibition — and he concludes that nevertheless Maharshal's words appear — see there:`,
    '7#א': `The strange ones. Rashi and Tur explained that they are not from a male but rather 'suspended from the ground.' Shach wrote: the same applies to from a male if there is no blood in them — even though they are strange, permitted:`,
    '7#ב': `To eat them. Shach wrote, and because of revulsion they are forbidden to a fastidious person (Shibbolei HaLeket in the name of the Geonim: if eggs remained under the hen three days — forbidden because of revulsion; and Beit Yosef and all the later authorities wrote anonymously to permit; and Shach and Peri Chadash brought proof like the Geonim's view):`,
  },
  'beer-hagolah': {
    '1#א': `Mishnah Chullin daf 117 and Kerisos daf 20:`,
    '1#ב': `Mishnah there daf 74:`,
    '1#ג': `There in the mishnah in Kerisos:`,
    '2#_': `Tur — the second resolution in Tosafot on that which is stated there in Chullin daf 64 that egg blood is forbidden:`,
    '3#א': `There in the baraita:`,
    '3#ב': `There as Dosetai taught, etc., like Rambam's view in chapter 3 of Laws of Forbidden Foods — that he disagrees with R' Yirmiyah; and so appears Rif's view, his teacher, and Tur in the name of the Geonim, Rav HaMagid, and other poskim:`,
    '4#א': `Tur in the name of the responsum of the Rosh, his father:`,
    '4#ב': `Hagahot Mordechai:`,
    '5#_': `Beit Yosef:`,
    '6#_': `Rashba in a responsum — because an egg's peel is no less than the measure of a peel:`,
    '7#א': `Baraita there daf 64, and Rashi explained that they are not from males and there is never an enclosed embryo in them — and so Rashba and Tur:`,
    '7#ב': `Tur, and like Rashba's view:`,
    '8#_': `Tosafot, Rosh, Rashba in his novellae, and Hagahot Maimoniyot chapter 3 of Forbidden Foods:`,
    '9#א': `Statement of Rav in Kerisos daf 21:`,
    '9#ב': `The Gemara's resolution there:`,
    '10#_': `There in Kerisos — and Rif and Rosh wrote it in chapter 8 of Chullin:`,
  },
  'beur-hagra': {
    '1#א': `And likewise, etc. Avodah Zarah and Hagahot Maimoniyot there:`,
    '1#ב': `But blood, etc. Kerisos 21b — the baraita teaches: blood, etc.:`,
    '2#_': `Blood of eggs, etc. Like both resolutions of Tosafot there s.v. vehu; and one may say it is d'rabbanan, etc.; and see that blood, etc.:`,
    '3#א': `If found, etc. Avodah Zarah; and according to this view there is no distinction between knot and outside the knot — in the chalmon everywhere forbidden, in the chelevon everywhere permitted. (Likkut) If found, etc. Rashi there 64b s.v. beitzim muzarot, etc.; and Rashba agreed and wrote that in them in every place one discards the blood and eats the rest (end of his words):`,
    '3#ב': `And some say, etc. This is the view of Rashi and the aforementioned Tosafot — that they do not distinguish; rather according to their view even in the chalmon it is forbidden only when found on the knot; only that Rosh and Rashba wrote one should be stringent like both views — and this is the 'some say' here:`,
    '3#ג': `And some are stringent, etc. A decree lest one find on the knot of the chalmon, which is forbidden d'oraisa, as explained above seif 2:`,
    '3#ד': `And from this spread, etc. Meaning even not on the knot — and see below. (Likkut) And from this spread, etc. But one may say for another reason that Ra'ah explained the opposite — in chelevon forbidden and chalmon permitted; and he wrote that this is what is stated there: Rav Geviha said to him, etc. — meaning Abaye is alone against everyone — see there where he elaborated; and so in Yesh Seder one should be stringent like his words — but it is forced; and also in the Jerusalem Talmud Tosafot brought there s.v. vehu, etc. — it is clear like Abaye's words — see there (end of his words):`,
    '4#א': `Eggs, etc. As above according to Rif's view — and he forbids their mixture even if it is only d'rabbanan, as written in Perek 3 (98a) — do not treat lightly, etc.:`,
    '4#ב': `But, etc. since, etc. Meaning from the Torah in min b'mino, as stated siman 98b and as written in Zevachim 79; and here there is further doubt whether it is forbidden at all; and even though in siman 110 we forbid in doubt and mixture — there it is d'oraisa, and here most poskim hold it is d'rabbanan; but without mixture one should be stringent, even though a rabbinic doubt is to be lenient, since some say it is d'oraisa:`,
    '5#_': `If found, etc. As written in end of this seif; and the same applies here to fish blood, etc. — and here too it is forbidden only from doubt, as Tosafot in Chullin and as explained above seif 2; and even though they were more stringent regarding it than regarding them if blood is found in them even though there is proof — nevertheless here they are equal; and see below:`,
    '6#_': `If found, etc. For without sauce it does not forbid more than roasting — the measure of a peel; and see Tosafot 96b s.v. im yesh; and Kereti 1 s.v. tipat, etc.; and like the view that roasted is bad with a peel; and see Tosafot there 96b s.v. ad; and Orach Chaim siman 467; and below siman 105; and here an egg's peel is no less than the measure of a peel:`,
    '7#_': `Provided, etc. As explained above seif 2 — that even if not in the place that forbids, the blood is forbidden d'rabbanan:`,
    '8#_': `Permitted, etc. Tosafot 64a s.v. simanim, etc.:`,
    '10#_': `And all, etc. For now we require everything that has proof — permitted; all the more so their mixture, where it is not recognizable — there is no concern of mar'it ayin:`,
  },
  kereti: {
    '1#_': `Chayah and fowl, etc. — their blood too is forbidden; only that which we say in Kerisos — an extra verse to forbid blood of koy — is according to those who say it is a species in itself; but according to what we rule, that it comes from beheimah and chayah — it is obvious it is forbidden, for whether chayah or beheimah blood is forbidden — so Peri Chadash; therefore Shulchan Aruch omitted it; and it is well written — only one may wonder why they wrote it regarding the hagahah:`,
    '2#_': `One is liable for it; and all the later authorities challenged — for below siman 86 seif 8 the Mechaber copied Rambam's wording that even tahor fowl eggs, even if formation began, are forbidden only d'rabbanan; and there it is not blood; and in truth if blood is recognizable — forbidden from the Torah; and see Peleti:`,
    '3#א': `And some are stringent, etc. On the knot of chelevon — a decree lest blood be found on the knot of chalmon, which is forbidden d'oraisa — Shach; and Peri Chadash wondered: from where do they come to decree a decree upon a decree; and this is in the Gemara, but in stringency that the poskim were stringent we find many decree upon decree; and a day-old egg — Maharshal's view is to be lenient, and so Peri Chadash:`,
    '3#ב': `And from this spread, etc. They wrote because of stringency; and the later authorities wrote from the law, for Ra'ah's view is the opposite — found on chelevon forbidden, for that is the essence of formation; and on chalmon permitted, for formation does not apply there; and Peri Chadash agreed with the law like Ra'ah, for according to him their reasons are sound — and see Peleti, for I clarified the Geonim's words with good reason, and from them one should not budge:`,
    '4#א': `And all this, etc. And if found or cooked and we do not know whether it was in the place that forbids — one should forbid — so Orach Chaim; and the later authorities agreed; and if mixed with other eggs — one in two permitted, for from the Torah it is nullified in the majority and it is only a rabbinic prohibition, and the doubt is to be lenient; and likewise even if roasted or cooked — if mixed one in two, likewise permitted; and see Peleti; and we do not say that blood in the egg is a prohibited attachment with the egg, and therefore sixty is required against the egg — for it is not attached to a derivative:`,
    '4#ב': `The egg alone. The wording 'alone' implies that if there is another egg too it is permitted; but Peri Chadash challenged and holds one in one is not called a mixture at all:`,
    '4#ג': `If it is, and if it became mixed not in its type — forbidden even in the majority, for not its type does not help majority like the essence; and it deals when mixed wet in wet, dry in dry; even not its type — from the Torah nullified in the majority — see above siman 109 what is written. And know that an egg in which there is a forbidden drop of blood — because 'he cast the flaw into the whole of it' — that egg that became mixed with others is min b'mino, for the flavor of blood, whatever amount, and the flavor of the egg in which it was cast are one flavor with the other eggs — so Peri Chadash:`,
    '5#_': `It appears to me it is permitted; and for us, who are stringent to forbid the egg in every case where blood is found — this too is terefah — Shach and Peri Chadash:`,
    '6#_': `Permitted — meaning the egg is not placed on a piece; but if placed on a piece — forbidden, for the egg is forbidden because of itself — 'he cast the flaw into the whole of it,' as below siman 105 — prohibited item on permitted; and for us, always sixty:`,
    '7#_': `Permitted to eat them — see Peleti. An egg upon which a rooster sat three days — forbidden; and this is when they are not 'suspended from the ground'; but when suspended from the ground — whoever is not fastidious may eat them; and 'three days' implies from above:`,
    '9#_': `Permitted — and Rambam in chapter 3 of Forbidden Foods ruled that even without scales it is permitted; and Ra'avad and Maggid Mishneh already challenged and strained themselves greatly; and I elaborated in my novellae — but because it is lengthy I did not copy it; and what is closer is that Rambam holds: if he puts in the blood and afterward drinks it in this — there is concern for mar'it ayin and scales are required; but if he puts in the blood and drinks it himself in this — there is no mar'it ayin and scales are not required. And this is what Rambam inferred: 'and drinks it' — meaning he puts it in; therefore they did not forbid — examine and requires further study:`,
    '10#_': `If it separated from him — and if recognition helps, like fish blood where Tosafot and Rashba disagreed — in a rabbinic matter we listen to be lenient:`,
  },
  peleti: {
    '2#_': `One is liable for it; and all the later authorities challenged — for below siman 86 seif 8 the Mechaber copied Rambam's wording that fowl eggs, even if formation began, are forbidden only d'rabbanan; and Maharshal explained, and Shach commented that the blood that turned to flesh left its prohibition of 'clot' and became like dust; and Peri Chadash commented that there was no blood there, only flesh, and because of anger this is not so — for formation never begins as blood; and one cannot find flesh without blood inside, as nature knows. Nevertheless there is difficulty: what difference — if blood alone, obviously forbidden; but if afterward absorbed in flesh — even if the piece is full of blood, all the more so if it did not separate — permitted, nullified by the piece of flesh; and this case is similar — the blood was absorbed in flesh. And the Rav in Beit Yosef wrote that from Rambam it appears blood of eggs is d'rabbanan — meaning he had to mention that if blood of formation without flesh is forbidden from the Torah — we learn that he holds this too is d'rabbanan; but we do not learn from the law itself; if so, there is no contradiction in Shulchan Aruch, for in truth he wrote in this siman forbidden from the Torah — and why mention more — requires further study:`,
    '3#_': `And from this spread the custom; and the later authorities wrote that not for this reason but from the law, for they were concerned for Ra'ah and his supporters, who hold: found on chelevon forbidden, on chalmon permitted; and Peri Chadash established the essential law like Ra'ah, for naturalists testified that all embryo formation is in the chelevon, and the chalmon is sometimes found inside the embryo's crop — see there. And in truth it is difficult for me to say that all the Geonim, Rif, and Rambam, who were leading naturalists, erred in this; therefore it appears that all of Ra'ah's proof from 'we learned the opposite' and Abaye interpreted for us — meaning Abaye erred like the Geonim — but in truth we learned the opposite; but this is extremely forced, since the correct view is like nature the opposite — why would Abaye argue thus without reason; he would always be the one who disputes whoever formed, investigating if it is possible; how would Abaye err in something obvious and further reverse the early authorities; the Gemara should have said 'and Abaye, who interpreted for us.' But it certainly appears the truth is as Ra'ah said: the essence of formation is in the chelevon, and the chalmon does not promote or demote embryo formation at all; but the matter is that most blood found in an egg comes for embryo formation, and otherwise blood is not common there at all. Blood comes to the egg before formation began, and afterward from it formation begins; therefore when blood is found in the chelevon, where the essence of embryo formation is — who will say formation already began? Perhaps it has not yet begun; and even if you say it began — perhaps it was here and found there; and from where do we know 'he cast the flaw into the whole of it'? But when found in the chalmon — there is no place of formation at all, and to attribute that it was not formation blood at all is meaningless; therefore we say that l'chatchila the blood was in the chelevon and also caused embryo formation — 'he cast the flaw into the whole of it'; and afterward when formation finished in the chelevon and the blood already did its work in formation and 'cast the flaw into the whole of it' — even the blood descends from chelevon to chalmon in its short connection to the chelevon, for certainly the chalmon too assists in forming the offspring — otherwise what is its nature and function, and it was not created in vain; if so, it makes sense that initially they differed like Ra'ah's view, but Abaye understood as written and therefore 'Abaye interpreted for us' must be the opposite in the chalmon; but in the chelevon we do not rely on formation, for when formation came the blood turned to flesh; but in the chalmon it is a remainder of formation that was in the chelevon — if so, this is all the poskim's view; and according to our version in the Jerusalem Talmud it is likewise — chalmon is more severe than chelevon; and such is essential:`,
    '4#_': `If roasted or cooked, etc. — and likewise they wrote that one in two may be lenient even if cooked, etc. And I do not know how to judge this — for all its prohibition is because of blood, since there is formation it is blood-flesh and not because of formation — for flesh of formation is certainly d'rabbanan; all prohibition is because of blood — so Tosafot and the poskim; if so, when cooked this is cooked blood, permitted from the Torah, and only rabbinic prohibition; and this blood is more severe than blood one is liable karet for, yet permitted in cooking — all the more so this; if so, there is never Torah doubt of prohibition here, only d'rabbanan — all the more so if we do not know it was in the forbidding place — it is a rabbinic doubt and to be lenient, and we do not establish prohibition; perhaps it was not in the forbidding place, and the egg stands on presumption of permission — requires examination, for the authors did not speak of this; and in my humble opinion regarding a cooked egg one may be lenient in every case — they are only rabbinic doubts — requires further study:`,
    '7#_': `Permitted to eat them. Beit Yosef wrote in the name of Shibbolei HaLeket in the name of the Geonim that if the egg remained under the hen three days — forbidden because of revulsion; and Beit Yosef wrote that Rashi contradicts, for it is written: a good soul shall eat them, even if it sat many days; and Shach wanted to distinguish between fastidious and not fastidious — and Peri Chadash spoke well, bringing from the Gemara Perek Makom SheNahagu that after three days eggs spoil and are unfit even for one who is not fastidious, as Rashi wrote there — within three days there is no great spoilage: a good soul shall eat them. But in truth the Geonim deal with eggs that are not 'suspended from the ground,' fit for laying and capable of slight formation — and the Gemara in Pesachim also deals with them: if suspended from the ground, why would the court inspect on the Festival; the rooster after three days because of loss of eggs — even if returned they are spoiled and will not succeed in hatching — a matter of desire; therefore in eggs fit for hatching the Gemara deals; and Rashi wrote explicitly on 'suspended from the ground' — even if it sat many days; and Beit Yosef who challenged Shibbolei HaLeket — that is regarding suspended from the ground — and he is correct; and in the Jerusalem Talmud Perek Makom SheNahagu it implies we require three days from above — see there:`,
  },
  'pitchei-teshuva': {
    '1#_': `And all this. Pri Chadash s.k. 10 elaborated that there is doubt in every place blood is found — even on the chalmon, perhaps the halacha is like Ra'ah, etc. — and according to his words there is no certainty except when found on the knot of the chalmon and also on the chelevon, when all forbid; and immediately after he wrote (and Be'er Heitev did not copy this) that Maharshal's leniency when found on the chalmon does not appear — see there, and his words contradict each other; and see Peri Megadim, who wrote that perhaps Shach retracted and holds the essential view is like the Geonim. And see Nachalat Azriel on Chullin daf 62, who explained his words: here the halacha is not like Maharshal, who is lenient on the chalmon because he holds the essential law is like Ra'ah; if so, one may be lenient b'dieved as truly Peri Chadash wrote (brought in Be'er Heitev of Mahari"t s.k. 3) — Shach wrote that all the later authorities hold not like him, only that chalmon is more severe; but in mixtures always be lenient because of doubt — see there. And what Be'er Heitev wrote in the name of Taz — a case that came before me, etc. — appears all this is according to his view below siman 98; but according to Shach there s.k. 8 it appears that not its type is forbidden; and so I found in the gloss of Yoreh De'ah of the Gaon R' Yaakov Berlin — but Peri Megadim wrote that Shach too may agree here — see there. [And see Levushei Sharad, who elaborated on this and several laws are explained there — see there:]`,
    '2#_': `And forgot and beat. Be'er Heitev in the name of Shach — and a practical difference also in a place, etc.; and see Sefer Tiferet Lemoshe, who wrote that it appears to me there is a practical difference for us with eggs that are 'suspended from the ground,' that only the blood is forbidden, as Shach s.k. 14 wrote — and if he beat with the blood, permitted; and it appears to me that even if there is doubt whether from a male or suspended from the ground, perhaps permitted in such a case; and even if you say forbidden, for according to those who say formation blood is d'oraisa — nevertheless if mixed one in one, and all the more so in two, it is nullified — it is a complete double doubt; and accordingly for us, since all eggs are doubt whether suspended from the ground, they are permitted in mixtures at least; however one may say 'suspended from the ground' is not so common that there would be blood as in male eggs, and the prohibition is more common — so it is not a complete double doubt — requires further study, end of his words — see there:`,
    '3#_': `Strange eggs. Pri Chadash; and see Tiferet Lemoshe, who wrote that the same applies to eggs found in the hen's innards in which blood is found — permitted, only discard the blood, for they too are never fertilized, as stated in chapter 1 of Beitzah daf 6 — requires further study for law — see there; and so I saw in the writings of the great Rav R' Dov — he ruled thus:`,
  },
  'rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 66 Shach s.k. 6) Even if it is a day-old egg. And in Tiferet Lemoshe he is in doubt regarding eggs found in the mother's innards — here one may say that since the egg is not fit at all to grow embryos, as stated in chapter 1 of Beitzah — it is like 'suspended from the ground,' permitted below seif 7:`,
    '2#_': `(Shach s.k. 10) Only in one it appears permitted. And Peri Chadash and Kereti and Menachem Tziyoni agreed that one may not permit except one in two:`,
    '3#_': `(Shach s.k. 13) And from the Rav's words it appears clear, etc. — it does not spread from piece to piece without sauce. A certain scholar challenged me: behold we rule that absorbed matter from a vessel goes out without sauce — since it has no flavor of its own, its flavor is not weak. If so, an egg's peel is like a vessel and is not comparable to piece to piece. And one may say: since we rule a peel has no measure and is minimal, perhaps the egg's peel itself is not absorbed through its entire thickness, only a little of its thickness where the peel is, and the rest is like two vessels touching one another, even though connected like one vessel — nevertheless it does not pass from side to side; for only from vessel to food does it go out without sauce, but vessel to vessel — no; and so we must say regarding food placed on a vessel, both hot — it appears a peel helps for a vessel like the law of roasting (see siman 108). And we do not say: since the vessels, it goes from this peel to the adjacent peel, and the entire vessel should be forbidden — for absorbed matter goes out from a vessel without sauce. Therefore from side to side in one vessel too it does not go out without sauce; even though if something hot touched from the back of the forbidden vessel and afterward cooked in that vessel we say the sauce extracts the absorbed matter and the food is forbidden; but with two vessels, each with sauce, touching one another — they do not forbid, as explained (end of siman 93). Here in one vessel from side to side is not called two vessels. One may say: specifically when there is sauce inside, for then the sauce enters the entire thickness of the vessel, therefore it extracts all in its thickness; and you do not find in one vessel from side to side without sauce — for the sauce inside spreads through its entire thickness; but whatever has no sauce inside is called two vessels regarding that absorbed matter does not pass side to side — as I explained:`,
    '4#_': `(There) Absorbed prohibition — the reason is that it is not called absorbed; one may say the egg's peel, which has no flavor of its own, is like a vessel — absorbed matter goes out from it without sauce:`,
    '5#_': `(There s.k. 14) End of his words — Tur and Rashi; and Torat Chatat wrote one may not permit except when known to be 'suspended from the ground,' such as the hen enclosed in a coop, or no rooster with her up to sixty houses; and Menachem Tziyoni wrote: or a river separates and there is no hawk; and Peri Chadash wrote it requires being enclosed one full day of its laying:`,
    '6#_': `(Seif 9) Such as that it has scales. The Talmud's wording: 'it has scales in it' — and Rashi wrote: there are scales in the blood — it appears at first glance blood mixed with scales; but to place scales next to the blood — not permitted; and perhaps one may take scales and put them in blood — no; rather that scales remained in it by themselves. See the sugya and Tosafot there — regarding blood of walkers on two [legs] it states 'it has scales in it' — why state this, for one could put scales in blood of walkers on two; and the viewer would think it is fish blood — rather it implies that does not help; however from Rambam's wording below (siman 87 seif 3) it appears here one may place scales next to it; and it also appears that if one cooked the blood, it is permitted to eat it according to Rambam (siman 87) — for even animal blood there is no Torah prohibition, for it is cooked blood, and it is like fowl flesh:`,
  },
  'kaf-hachayim': {
    '1#_': `(1) [Seif 1] Blood of beheimah, chayah, and fowl, etc.; and likewise blood of koy — one is liable. Kerisos 21a; Tur and Beit Yosef; Levush; Shach s.k. 1; Peri Chadash note 1. In Levush note 1 and this is for those who say it is a species in itself; but for us, who rule it is a doubt — chayah or beheimah — in any case one is liable for its blood. Peri Chadash there; Orach Chaim note 2; and see above siman 64 note 4.`,
    '2#_': `(2) A place where they place salt in nevelah blood, and if a person says to bring him salt made in permissibility from outside — it is forbidden in its visible form, such as to immerse an egg or radish in it; but permitted to put it in a cooked dish l'chatchila; and if there is something else in the salt along with the blood — it is 'this and this cause' and permitted even to eat the salt in its visible form; and all such cases we rely on a non-Jewish taste-tester. Ram"a of Panu siman 27; Knesset HaGedolah in Hagahat Tur note 1; Peri Chadash below siman 105 note 58; Lev HaPanim there note 58; in Levush there note 58; Shvut Yaakov note 1; end of this siman from Mechaber note 12; Zivchei Tzedek note 2; and see below in the hagahah to siman 134 and our words there.`,
    '3#_': `(3) Musk, which they call musk — it is from a chayah whose name is known, and it has a kind of hump on its neck where blood first collects like blood and afterward dries and becomes musk. Some say it is forbidden to eat because of concern for blood; some say permitted, for it is mere secretion; and even though its beginning was blood — afterward. Now we follow; and as Knesset HaGedolah elaborated in Hagahat Tur note 2 and concludes: for practical halacha the common people are accustomed to permit musk in eating, and daily practice is to throw musk into broth or sweets for good fragrance — see there. And likewise in the indexes at the end of Kol Bo Levi siman 106. And likewise Taz in Orach Chaim siman 216 s.k. 2; Peri Chadash in responsa Mayim Chayim siman 10 regarding Orach Chaim there note 2; Shvut Yaakov in this siman note 2; from Mechaber note 11; Zivchei Tzedek note 3; and see further what we elaborated in Orach Chaim siman 216 notes 21–22 — see there.`,
    '4#_': `(4) Nevertheless civet, which is the sweat of the civet cat — it appears to me forbidden to all for eating, for one cannot say it dried, for it remains moist; and accordingly one must be careful not to eat civet or that it was put in foods; and likewise forbidden to smell it and recite a blessing, as Shaarei Dura wrote: everything forbidden in eating — one does not smell or bless on it; and Knesset HaGedolah in Hagahat Tur in Orach Chaim there note 8; and likewise in the indexes at the end of Kol Bo Levi there. And Beit Din — Shvut Yaakov in this siman at the aforementioned note 4. However, what is written that everything forbidden in eating — one does not smell or bless on it — is not the poskim's view; rather even if forbidden in eating, one may bless and smell it, since it is not forbidden in benefit — as we wrote in our words in Orach Chaim there note 23 — see there.`,
    '5#_': `(5) There. But blood of fish and locusts is permitted — see below seif 9.`,
    '6#_': `(6) [Seif 3] If a speck of blood is found upon it, one discards the blood, etc. In the holy community of Hebron 5660 they practiced: if a speck of blood is found on the chalmon, they remove it with cotton wool; and if the soft membrane on the chalmon remains whole, they eat the egg — from Mechaber note 2; and he wrote this is according to Shulchan Aruch's ruling in this law that specifically when found in the chalmon it forbids the whole; therefore if the speck was removed and the membrane on the chalmon remained whole, it proves the blood was in the chelevon — discard the blood and the rest is permitted, as the Geonim wrote and Maran ruled. And likewise in note 4 — except he concluded: this is to instruct others; however a scrupulous person should be stringent for himself and forbid any egg in which blood is found in any manner — and so the wholesome ones practiced, and many — end of his words. And Beit Din — Zivchei Tzedek note 5 wrote: and we in our place, from the day we established our view, thus we practice for every questioner — to forbid in every case the entire egg, whether in chalmon or chelevon — end of his words; and likewise Ben Ish Chai, Parashat Tahorot note 5 — such is the custom in Baghdad — see there — to forbid in every manner. However, where they already practiced like the custom of Hebron and Shulchan Aruch's ruling — one does not treat them lightly; only a scrupulous person should be stringent for himself as mentioned; and it appears that also one who comes to ask in that place where they practice like Shulchan Aruch — one informs him that some are stringent, for perhaps he will separate himself if he is scrupulous. And see below note 14.`,
    '7#_': `(7) In time of distress or b'dieved one may rely on the Geonim to be lenient when found in the chelevon outside the knot. Chessed David on the Tosefta chapter 9 of Terumot; Orach Chaim note 5; Zivchei Tzedek note 6; Ben Ish Chai there. And behold the matter of the knot is known — the chelevon has a knot at the blunt head, a matter grasped from the male's seed, recognizable to the eye as more tied; and likewise the chalmon has a knot — like a round crown the width of a large lentil; and according to the Geonim embryo formation is from the chalmon, therefore they are more stringent on the chalmon; and such is Maran's view. But Ra'ah and Rokeach hold embryo formation is from the chelevon, therefore they are more stringent on the chelevon. Ben Ish Chai there; and this is from Beit Yosef's words; and see below note 14.`,
    '8#_': `(8) And Kol Bo wrote that when blood is found in an egg in the place that forbids it — even if it is a day-old egg it is forbidden, for the Sages did not distinguish. Beit Yosef; Torat Chesed general rule 62 end of din 1; and he wrote such is the custom — Shach s.k. 6; Pri Chadash note 6; and he concludes there — Shach: the later authorities' view appears that one should not distinguish; and it is implied he means that wherever blood is found in a day-old egg one should forbid. However Menachem Tziyoni on Torat Chesed there note 5 wrote: if found on the chelevon not at the place of the knot — one may permit in a day-old egg — not like Shach; and Beit Din — Pri Chadash there — wrote one may lean thus where there is financial loss in the matter.`,
    '9#_': `(9) However, certainly when blood is found in a day-old egg it is only d'rabbanan, and a practical difference to be lenient in its doubt when it fell into liquid and one does not know if there are sixty, and similar cases. Pri Chadash there; Zivchei Tzedek note 7.`,
    '10#_': `(10) But complete eggs in the mother's innards — even if the outer shell was completed — one may be lenient. Orach Chaim note 6 in the name of Rashba; however Pri Chadash end of note 10 wrote: perhaps they decreed on it like a day-old egg. And Beit Din — Mechaber note 10; Zivchei Tzedek note 9; however it is d'rabbanan. Pri Chadash there.`,
  },
  'mateh-yehonatan': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 66 seif 4 in the hagahah) And all this when we know the blood was in the place that forbids it, etc. See Shach and Taz — some want to say that even one in one is permitted because blood of eggs is d'rabbanan — see there; and Sefer Lechem challenged: behold above seif 1 the Rav ruled anonymously in the Mechaber's words that formation blood is forbidden d'oraisa — see there. And one may say: behold here it deals certainly that one discards the blood outside — only there is doubt whether it was in the place of prohibition — 'he cast the flaw into the whole of it' or not; if so, what the Mechaber wrote in seif 1 that formation blood is forbidden d'oraisa — is the blood itself; but that which we say 'he cast the flaw into the whole of it' is not a Torah prohibition, only a rabbinic doubt:`,
  },
  'yad-avraham': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 66 seif 2) Blood of eggs — if known that it is marukem ha'afroach one is liable; but if not known, etc. — the Sages forbade it. Meaning it is not forbidden from the Torah unless the form of the embryo is recognizable in this blood; but if the form of the embryo is not recognizable, even if found in the chalmon — according to Shulchan Aruch seif 3 the entire egg is forbidden because we are concerned for formation; nevertheless its prohibition is d'rabbanan — and this is the first resolution in Tosafot that Rosh in Perek 9 and Beit Yosef bring. And Tur's wording is slightly different: 'but if it is known that it is not marukem ha'afroach, etc. — forbidden by rabbinic words' — meaning in the chelevon according to Shulchan Aruch's view, that we are not concerned for formation there at all; nevertheless forbidden d'rabbanan. It is proven from this that in the chalmon, where we are concerned for formation, it is forbidden d'oraisa — and this is the second resolution in Tosafot and Rosh; only that Tur adopted the stringency of both — see there and in Beit Yosef. And I wrote this to exclude what Be'er HaGolah wrote — that Shulchan Aruch's words are like Tur's second resolution; and it is not so, for I already clarified the difference between them; and likewise Taz — Shulchan Aruch ruled like the first resolution in Tosafot that in every place its prohibition is d'rabbanan — requires further study. And therefore one may be lenient in mixtures min b'mino in the majority where there is doubt whether the blood was in the place that forbids it; only if certainly in the chalmon — according to Magen Avraham and Levushei Serad it is not called sixty, perhaps we hold like Ra'ah, who is alone against those who forbid in the chalmon; but it appears that in time of distress it is considered such a case for doubt, as siman 110; and in a day-old egg Magen Avraham is lenient on the chelevon on the sides:`,
    '2#_': `(Seif 10) Human blood, etc. — between the teeth one sucks it out. Magen Avraham in the name of Beit Shmuel: the reason — even though visible to him, recognizable that it came from the person, and there is no mar'it ayin; and similarly Tosafot on Shabbos (daf 62) and Sefer Sha'ar HaMelech, Laws of Yom Tov — from reasoning of the soul; and see Tosafot and Rosh on Eruvin (87b) and Levush wrote: it appears to me like Rashi in Ketubot — where visible to him, forbidden; and Magen Avraham already preceded at the end of general rule 62. And see what Peri Megadim wrote here, that it contradicts what he wrote in siman 5; and see what is written in siman 87:`,
    '3#_': `(There in the hagahah) And all blood of fish and human blood — since by law permitted, do not forbid their mixture. Meaning when mixed in a manner where there is no mar'it ayin, for they are not visible — for otherwise forbidden; and so Rashba, and Beit Yosef brings it. And now it is explained regarding bread in the previous seif — if he bit and blood came on the bread, he must scrape it, even though he eats it through mixture; nevertheless it is visible:`,
  },
  'yad-ephraim': {
    '1#_': `Permitted to eat roasted eggs. And Maharachav wrote in Likutei Torah in Sha'ar HaMitzvot, Parashat Acharei, regarding a roasted egg — I saw my teacher that he would swallow it roasted without examining whether there is blood, and he was not concerned for the stringent view not to swallow a roasted egg; and he brought proof from the Talmud in many places regarding swallowing an egg alive or roasted, and the incident of R' Akiva who was fasting on erev Shabbos and they brought him a peeled egg and he swallowed it, and many such; and the reason — because it is a double doubt and d'rabbanan — end of his words; and Mahar Chasdav brought it and analyzed 'without examining' — implying there is examination; whereas in Hagahot Maimoniyot that he brought Beit Yosef, it implies there is no examination in roasting, as Maran wrote in this law; and Lechem wrote to distinguish between hard roasted eggs called 'ripidan,' where if there is blood it might be seen immediately — if so, no proof from the Talmud, for perhaps they examined; and it is forced to say his intent was that he did not command to empty them first, etc. (meaning as some write that some people empty into a bowl first and return to the shell, seal the hole, and roast — see there); and see there he also explained 'it is a double doubt d'rabbanan'; and concludes: in revelation it was revealed — our holy master the Ari said one need not be concerned for the stringency of people who write to empty first — see there. And see there he also wrote that certainly in the Rav's house, when making a dish or frying they would look if there is blood — emptying the whole egg from its shell to cook it, etc. — not so in swallowing — see there. And I did not understand the distinction, for certainly for making a dish, which is through mixture, one must be more stringent; therefore it certainly appears that after roasting it is far that examination would help, and that was the stringent view not to swallow a roasted egg; and if so, as Mahar Chasdav wrote, they should have examined and swallowed; and likewise proof from the Talmud. And what 'without examining' means — that he was not concerned for the stringent view at all, and even as a stringency to examine lest something be recognizable after roasting — he did not do; therefore since he needed to swallow the egg after roasting, he was not concerned; but when they needed to cook or fry, where it is easy to check — it certainly appears he did as Mahar Chasdav wrote — see Tiferet Lemoshe, who wrote regarding an egg in the hen's innards in which blood is found — requires further study; and likewise Pri Chadash wrote: perhaps they decreed on it like a day-old egg:`,
    '2#_': `Fish blood, etc. And he wrote in responsa Nachalat Sheva — regarding blood found in milk, the milk is forbidden — see there; and likewise in responsa Chinuch, Beit Yosef siman 38 — blood found in a pot or vessel into which milk was milked from an animal — the milk is forbidden; therefore milk that tends to redness is forbidden; and if they made butter, one may sell it to a non-Jew in a manner that there is no concern he will return and sell it to an Israelite; and likewise vessels used with hot food are forbidden — see there:`,
  },
};



